title The loops checked on metal -- sound machinery, and a route that points somewhere else
prompt Let me know if our outer and inner recursion loops are still satisfactory for continuing.
think Read the artifacts rather than answering from memory of them: the seed at recursion-prompts/seed/autonomous-loop.seed.md, the outer loop printed by tools/launch-claude-season.rish, and the census witness that guards the cellar. A question about whether machinery still works is answered by running it, not by recalling that it once did.
think Checked the mechanics that fail silently rather than loudly, since those are the ones a reading pass misses. All 27 council-rota docs exist on disk -- a missing one would make a lap's deep-read quietly no-op. Every doc the two prompts name by path exists, including the itineraries, the microkernel study, the jq render filter, and the jail elder. The census witness is GREEN.
think Checked the stop rule's own footing, because the loop's whole safety rests on it. The sentinel .loop-gates-only is ignored by .gitignore line 8, the root /* catch-all, so a send's git add -A can never commit it and turn one lap's pause into a permanent one. That is the failure mode the file sentinel was chosen to avoid in the first place, and it holds.
think Compared the seed's six custody gates against crux/REMEMBER.md's own list and they match one for one. The card shows gate 1 struck through as done for the AHOY3 push, and the card's own text still reserves future seed refreshes to Keaton's word, so the seed listing it as live is correct rather than stale.
obs The machinery is sound. The route is not. Both loop prompts hardcode the Lindy-priority Microkernel Target double-seat as the lead, so a loop launched right now would advance microkernel study rungs and never see DISC1. The arc named and opened this very session is invisible to the loop that would supposedly continue it. That is the one finding that actually answers the question asked.
obs This is drift of exactly the kind the script warns about in its own header -- to evolve the loop, fill a fresh version in the cellar rather than editing the printed lines from memory. The route slot is a {{slot}} in the seed and a hardcoded paragraph in the filled versions, so the seed is fine and the versions have aged.
obs A second, smaller finding from this round's own work: vendor/pqclean is a new submodule dependency. A fresh jail clone without --recurse-submodules leaves the vendored parity roster RED, and the loop's Standfast rule would book that as a red and stop the line for an uninitialized checkout rather than a defect. The oracle witness already fails loudly with a message naming the submodule, which is the right behavior; what is missing is the loop knowing that this particular red is an environment fact.
obs Named but did not fix. Whether DISC outranks the Microkernel Target in the loop's standing priority is a Lindy-ordering call, and re-aiming a standing route is not the same as taking the next lap on it. Reported the state and left the ordering to Keaton, per the counsel practice.
obs One reproducibility note worth recording rather than acting on: pqclean is pinned to a master-branch SHA where monocypher is pinned to the 4.0.3 tag. The recorded SHA is what a fresh init fetches, so it is reproducible today; a tag or a release pin would be sturdier over years.
file session-logs/20260821-031410_the-loops-checked-and-the-route-that-points-elsewhere.kyri the reading -- machinery GREEN, route stale, nothing in the tree changed
recommend check-in The loops will run and will run correctly; they will run toward the microkernel study rather than toward DISC1. Re-aiming wants one fresh filled version in recursion-prompts/versions/ naming DISC as the lead and the submodule init as a precondition -- and which arc leads is Keaton's ordering call, not mine.
